TITLE:: BusRecorder
summary:: record any set of buses to disk, and a window to do it from
categories:: Libraries>BusRecorder
related:: Classes/BusRecorderSource, Classes/BusRecorderGUI, Classes/BusManager, Classes/DiskOut

DESCRIPTION::
A list of sources - buses, by index or by BusManager name - and a way of
writing them to disk however you ask: one multichannel file, stereo pairs, one
file per source, one file per channel. Every file in a take starts on the same
sample.

code::
s.boot;
BusRecorder.add(\main, 0, 2);
BusRecorder.add(\drums, 16, 2);
BusRecorder.mode = \sources;
BusRecorder.fileName = "take";
BusRecorder.record;                  // take_001_main.wav, take_001_drums.wav
BusRecorder.stop;

BusRecorder.gui;
::

Everything is on the class: there is one recorder per session, the way there is
one server, and no instance to keep hold of.

It depends on nothing. When strong::BusManager:: is installed alongside it, a
source can be added by bus name and follows that name from then on - see
link::#Sources:: below.

subsection:: Sources

A source is a name and a way of finding a bus. Nothing is ever allocated or
freed: the recorder only reads, which is what makes it safe to point at
anything.

table::
## strong::kind:: || strong::made by:: || strong::found again by::
## code::\index:: || code::add:: with a number, code::addIndex:: || the index you gave
## code::\name:: || code::add:: with a symbol, code::addName:: || asking BusManager, whenever its buses change
## code::\bus:: || code::addBus:: || the Bus itself
::

code::
BusRecorder.add(\main, 0, 2);             // a bus index and a channel count
BusRecorder.addIndex(16, 2);              // the same, named bus16 for you
BusRecorder.add(\drums);                  // a BusManager name; the source is called drums too
BusRecorder.add(\kick, \drums, 1);        // the same bus, first channel only
BusRecorder.addBus(Bus.audio(s, 2));      // a Bus something else made
::

A source added by name follows it, even in the middle of a take. A name
BusManager does not know leaves the source in the list but unresolved: it shows
a code::?:: in the window and is left out of the take until the name turns up.

subsection:: The take

code::mode:: decides how the armed sources' channels are cut into files:

table::
## strong::mode:: || strong::what you get::
## code::\single:: || every channel of every source in one multichannel file
## code::\pairs:: || the same channels, cut into stereo files two at a time
## code::\sources:: || one file per source, however many channels it has
## code::\mono:: || one file per channel
::

A file is named after the take - code::fileName::, then the take number - with
the sources in it appended: by name, or by bus index when code::label:: is
code::\index::. A file holding part of a wider source says which part, counting
from 1: code::take_001_quad_3-4.wav::.

code::
BusRecorder.nextPaths;      // what the next take would write
BusRecorder.record;
BusRecorder.pause;
BusRecorder.resume;
BusRecorder.stop;
BusRecorder.record(30);     // stops itself after thirty seconds
::

Which sources go in and what they are called is decided when code::record::
runs; anything changed afterwards is for the next take.

subsection:: Metering and listening

Metering is one small synth per source replying fifteen times a second. It is
off until something asks for it: opening the window turns it on, closing it
turns it off again.

code::
BusRecorder.meter_(true);
BusRecorder.level(\drums);          // the loudest channel right now
BusRecorder.at(\drums).levels;      // one per channel

BusRecorder.listen(\drums);         // hear it on the monitor output
BusRecorder.listenOff;
::

One source is listened to at a time. Listening to a source that overlaps the
monitor output is refused rather than played, since that is a feedback loop.

subsection:: Server restarts

After a boot the synth definitions are sent again and every source added by
name looks its bus up again. cmd-period takes every node, the disk synths
included: the files are closed so they get their headers, and the post window
says the take was cut short. If the server itself dies with a take open, the
files are left without headers.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: add
Add a source. What code::bus:: is decides how it is found.
ARGUMENT:: name
a Symbol. With code::bus:: nil, also the BusManager name to follow.
ARGUMENT:: bus
an Integer (a bus index), a Symbol (a BusManager name), a link::Classes/Bus::, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: channels
how many channels to record, from the start of the bus. 2 for an index; all of them for a name or a Bus.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/BusRecorderSource::.

METHOD:: addIndex
A bare index and a channel count. Named code::bus16:: and so on unless told otherwise.

METHOD:: addName
A BusManager name, followed from then on. Kept but unresolved if BusManager does
not know it, or is not installed.

METHOD:: addBus
A link::Classes/Bus:: something else made, read where it is. Refused for a control bus.

METHOD:: remove
Take a source out. Nothing is freed.

METHOD:: rename
Give a source a different name. Refused if the new name is taken.

METHOD:: arm
METHOD:: disarm
METHOD:: isArmed
Whether a source goes into the next take. A disarmed source stays in the list,
metered and listenable.

METHOD:: clear
Empty the list. A take in progress carries on; its files were planned when it started.

METHOD:: at
The link::Classes/BusRecorderSource:: under a name, or nil.

METHOD:: includes
Whether a name is in the list.

METHOD:: names
Every name, in the order they were added.

METHOD:: size
How many sources there are.

METHOD:: sources
Every link::Classes/BusRecorderSource::, in order.

METHOD:: record
Start a take. Every file gets its own disk synth, started in one bundle.
ARGUMENT:: dur
seconds after which it stops itself. Nil to record until code::stop::.

METHOD:: stop
End the take: the synths are freed, then the files closed so they get their
headers. The post window lists what was written.

METHOD:: pause
METHOD:: resume
Hold the disk synths. Nothing is written while paused; the file skips the gap.

METHOD:: recording
METHOD:: paused
Whether a take is running, and whether it is held.

METHOD:: duration
Seconds written so far, or the length of the last take.

METHOD:: files
The paths of the take in progress, or the last one.

METHOD:: plan
Which channels go into which file: a list of files, each a list of
code::[source, channel]:: pairs. Only armed, resolved sources take part.

METHOD:: nextPaths
The files the next take would write, so you can check before you press.

METHOD:: nextTake
The take number the next take gets: one more than the highest in the folder under this name.

METHOD:: mode
How a take is split into files: code::\single::, code::\pairs::, code::\sources:: or code::\mono::. code::\single:: by default.

METHOD:: label
What a file is labelled with: code::\name::, the source's name, or code::\index::, the index of its bus as code::bus16::. code::\name:: by default.

METHOD:: numbering
code::\take:: for a three digit take number, code::\timestamp:: for the date and time, code::\none:: for neither - and the next take overwrites. code::\take:: by default.

METHOD:: fileName
The first part of every file name. code::"rec":: by default, and when set to nothing.

METHOD:: dir
Where the files go. SuperCollider's own recordings folder unless set. Made when the first take starts.

METHOD:: headerFormat
code::"wav"::, code::"aiff"::, or code::"caf"::. code::"wav":: by default.

METHOD:: sampleFormat
code::"int16"::, code::"int24"::, or code::"float"::. code::"int24":: by default.

METHOD:: bufSize
Frames of disk buffer per file, as link::Classes/Server#-recBufSize::. 65536 by default.

METHOD:: level
The loudest channel of a source right now. 0 unless metering is on.

METHOD:: meter
Whether the meters are running. Off by default; the window turns it on while it is open.

METHOD:: listen
Route one source to the monitor output so you can hear it. Starting another
stops the first. Refused for an unresolved source, and for one overlapping the
monitor output.

METHOD:: listenOff
Stop listening.

METHOD:: isListeningTo
Whether that name is the one being listened to.

METHOD:: monitoring
The source being listened to, or nil.

METHOD:: monitorOut
Where listening goes. 0 by default. Moving it onto the source being listened to stops the monitor rather than making a loop.

METHOD:: monitorAmp
Monitor level, 0 to 2. 0.5 by default.

METHOD:: saveLayout
METHOD:: loadLayout
The sources and every setting, written to one code::.scd:: file and read back.
A bare name lands in the repository's code::layouts:: folder. Loading replaces
everything. Nothing is ever restored automatically.

METHOD:: layouts
The layout files on disk, newest first - what the window's dropdown offers.

METHOD:: layoutDir
Where they live.

METHOD:: server
The server the sources are read on. link::Classes/Server::.default unless set.
Setting it stops a take and empties the list.

METHOD:: gui
Open the window, or bring it to the front. link::Classes/BusRecorderGUI::.

METHOD:: meterRate
How many times a second a meter reports. 15.


EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;

// two sources, and a window to watch them in
BusRecorder.add(\main, 0, 2);
BusRecorder.add(\side, 2, 2);
BusRecorder.gui;

// something to look at
~noise = { Out.ar(2, PinkNoise.ar(0.1) ! 2) }.play;

BusRecorder.level(\side);       // moving
BusRecorder.listen(\side);      // and now audible
BusRecorder.listenOff;

// how the take is written
BusRecorder.fileName = "demo";
BusRecorder.mode = \sources;
BusRecorder.nextPaths;          // demo_001_main.wav, demo_001_side.wav

// a take
BusRecorder.record(5);          // stops itself
BusRecorder.files;

~noise.free;

// save the lot, and get it back
BusRecorder.saveLayout("tonight");
BusRecorder.clear;
BusRecorder.loadLayout("tonight");
::

There is a longer walkthrough in code::examples/bus-recorder-demo.scd:: in the
repository.
